WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
A secure LLM architecture names every source of instruction, data, identity, and action.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.600
Users provide prompts.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.700
System and developer instructions define trusted application behavior.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:28.480
Retrieved documents, uploaded files, conversation history, tool outputs, SDK responses, provider messages, vector stores, application databases, logs, and telemetry all contribute information with different trust levels.

5
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:36.020
The integration should make those sources explicit instead of merging them into one undifferentiated block of text.

6
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:46.060
A trust boundary is crossed when data, instructions, credentials, identity, or control moves between components with different authority.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.280
A user prompt is not the same as a trusted system rule.

8
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.220
A retrieved document is not the same as application configuration.

9
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.240
A hosted provider is not the same boundary as a local runtime.

10
00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.500
A model response is not the same as a verified business decision.

11
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.200
These distinctions shape what the application may trust, store, display, or act on.

12
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.800
Trusted configuration must be separated from untrusted content.

13
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:21.340
System instructions, allowed tools, retrieval policies, credential scopes, and authorization rules should live in application-controlled code or configuration.

14
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:34.480
Untrusted text from users, files, websites, tickets, documents, or tool outputs can provide evidence or intent, but it should not grant itself authority over data, tools, policies, or permissions.

15
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.980
Natural language may look commanding without being entitled to control the system.

16
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:43.020
Boundary-aware design also improves review and operations.

17
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:54.720
When teams can see where prompts are built, where credentials are used, where retrieval filters run, where tools are authorized, and where outputs are validated, they can test and monitor the right points.

18
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:58.580
Without those boundaries, LLM behavior can feel mysterious.

19
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:05.880
With them, the integration becomes a normal security-sensitive application flow that can be reviewed, logged, and improved.
